# Corpus tiers. One line per tier: the lowest PHP that tier's corpus runs on,
# then the apps that corpus trains.
#
#   <floor> <app> <app> ...
#
# Membership is not listed here: scripts/pgo_tiers.py computes it from matrix.json
# (a version belongs to the tier with the greatest floor <= it), and also derives
# the release Composer resolves against, the builder image and the image tag. The
# floor column only says where the boundaries fall. Most are set by what the
# frameworks support; the two newest by which PrestaShop trains which PHP.
#
# The app column is the one declaration of what a tier trains
# (`pgo_tiers.py apps-of <floor>`). Dockerfile.corpus installs Drupal only where
# it is listed, corpus/verify.sh serves exactly these apps, and tests/test-corpus.sh
# expects exactly these in /corpus. Apps are named after their corpus/<app>/
# directory and listed in the order MANIFEST records them. Drupal 11.4.8 (php
# >=8.3) trains the 8.4 and 8.5 tiers only, so the newest PHP lines are profiled on
# the CMS stack too.
#
# Per tier:
#
#   8.5  PrestaShop 9.2.0 trains this tier alone. Laravel 12, Symfony 7 and
#        WordPress are the 8.2 tier's pins (same releases, same locks); only the
#        root php constraint and config.platform.php in their composer.json move
#        to the floor (^8.5 / 8.5.11). Drupal 11.4.8 and drush 13 resolve against
#        the same platform.
#   8.4  PrestaShop 8.2.8 and the same pins for the rest, plus Drupal 11.4.8
#        resolved against 8.4.26. The 8.4/8.5 split is not a framework limit; the
#        composer.json floor constraint keeps the corpus from also running one
#        version down, which tests/test-corpus-tiers.sh's below-floor control
#        relies on.
#   8.2  Laravel 12 (php ^8.2), Symfony 7 (php >=8.2), WordPress 7.0 and
#        PrestaShop 8.2.8 (see 8.1). Serves 8.2 and 8.3. Laravel 13 needs 8.3, so
#        8.2 is where the Laravel 12 line stops.
#   8.1  Laravel 10 (php ^8.1) and Symfony 6.4 (php >=8.1) are the newest that
#        install on 8.1; Laravel 11 and Symfony 7 both require 8.2. WordPress 7.0
#        still applies (it wants 7.4). PrestaShop 8.2.8 trains this tier and every
#        one up to 8.4 (7.2, 8.1, 8.2, 8.4): the devdocs say 8.2 supports php up to
#        8.1, but PS 8.2 has run on PHP 8.4 in production for a long time, so the
#        pins deliberately go beyond the docs. (The installer's numeric cap,
#        install_version.php's _PS_INSTALL_MAXIMUM_PHP_VERSION_ID_, is 81099 --
#        php 8.10.99, a typo for 80199 -- so it blocks nothing below php 8.10; the
#        CLI install runs on 8.4 as shipped.)
#   7.2  Laravel 6 (php ^7.2.5), Symfony 5.4 (php >=7.2.5, the newest Symfony that
#        runs on PHP 7 at all) and WordPress 6.9, the last line whose
#        $required_php_version (7.2.24) is below PHP 7.2.34. WordPress 7.0
#        requires 7.4, so a 7.4 floor would be unusable on 7.2 and 7.3.
#   7.1  Laravel 5.8 (php ^7.1.3), Symfony 4.4 (php >=7.1.3, the last Symfony
#        below the 7.2.5 floor 5.4 sets) and WordPress 6.5 (7.0.0, the last line
#        below WordPress 6.6's 7.2.24), with the 2.1 SQLite drop-in (php 7.0).
#   7.0  Laravel 5.5 (php >=7.0.0), Symfony 3.4 (php >=7.0.8, the last branch
#        below Symfony 4.4's 7.1.3), and the same WordPress 6.5 and 2.1 drop-in.
#        This tier needs PHP 7.0 to link Debian's libsqlite (3.46.1) rather than
#        its bundled 3.14.2: the drop-in writes its type cache with UPSERT, which
#        SQLite gained in 3.24, and WordPress otherwise dies at CREATE TABLE
#        wp_users. php/system-libs.lock asserts the linkage per image.
8.5 laravel prestashop symfony wordpress drupal
8.4 laravel prestashop symfony wordpress drupal
8.2 laravel prestashop symfony wordpress
8.1 laravel prestashop symfony wordpress
7.2 laravel prestashop symfony wordpress
7.1 laravel prestashop symfony wordpress
7.0 laravel prestashop symfony wordpress
